PrepFlow
Database Design Document

Version: v1.0

Status: Draft

Author: Tejas Nere

1. Purpose

This document defines the database architecture of PrepFlow.

The database is designed to be completely dynamic, scalable, and future-ready.

The primary objective is to ensure that administrators can manage platform content without modifying application source code.

2. Database Philosophy

PrepFlow follows a data-driven architecture.

The frontend should never contain hardcoded learning resources.

All platform content must come from the database.

If tomorrow a new resource, tag, metadata field, or chapter is added, no frontend code should require modification.

3. Database Technology

Database

MongoDB Atlas

File Storage

Supabase Storage

Authentication

(Admin V1)

Future

Student Authentication

4. Collections

The platform initially contains the following collections.

Users
Subjects
Chapters
Resources
Progress
Tags
Resource Types

Future collections may be added without affecting the existing architecture.

5. Users Collection

Stores all user information.

Example fields:

Name
Email
Role
Profile Image
Created Date
Last Login

Roles:

Student

Administrator

Future:

Moderator

6. Subjects Collection

Each document represents one subject.

Example:

Physics

Mathematics

Chemistry

Each subject contains:

Name
Description
Display Order
Status
Icon
Color (Optional)
Metadata
7. Chapters Collection

Every chapter belongs to one subject.

Each chapter contains:

Subject ID
Chapter Name
Description
Display Order
Status
Metadata
Tags

The chapter should NOT directly store resources.

Resources are stored separately.

8. Resources Collection

Every learning resource belongs to one chapter.

Examples:

Playlist

Notes

Formula Sheet

PYQs

Future:

Quiz

Mind Map

Mock Test

Flashcards

Assignments

Books

Each resource contains:

Chapter ID
Resource Type
Title
Description
URL
Display Order
Visibility
Metadata
Tags
9. Progress Collection

Stores learning progress.

Each document contains:

User ID
Resource ID
Completion Status
Completed At
Last Updated

Chapter progress should never be stored manually.

It should always be calculated.

10. Tags Collection

Tags are dynamic.

Administrators can create unlimited tags.

Examples:

High Weightage

Important

Beginner

Conceptual

Revision

Scoring

The frontend should never hardcode tags.

11. Resource Types Collection

Resource types should also be dynamic.

Default examples:

Playlist

Notes

Formula Sheet

PYQs

Future:

Quiz

Assignment

Mock Test

Mind Map

Flashcards

Administrators should be able to create future resource types.

12. Metadata Philosophy

Metadata should remain completely dynamic.

Nothing should be fixed.

Example metadata:

Estimated Time

Difficulty

Teacher

Language

Importance

Batch

Expected Questions

Future metadata should require zero code changes.

13. Relationships

Subject

↓

Multiple Chapters

↓

Multiple Resources

↓

Student Progress

Resources should never be embedded inside chapter documents.

Instead, they should be linked using IDs.

14. File Storage

Only metadata is stored inside MongoDB.

Actual files remain inside Supabase Storage.

MongoDB stores:

Title

File URL

Resource Information

Supabase stores:

PDF

Images

Documents

Formula Sheets

15. Progress Calculation

Progress should always be calculated.

Resource Completion

↓

Chapter Completion

↓

Subject Completion

↓

Overall Completion

The database should never manually store duplicate progress values.

16. Visibility

Every document should support visibility.

Draft

Published

Hidden

Students only receive Published content.

17. Ordering

Everything displayed on the platform should support ordering.

Subjects

Chapters

Resources

Tags

Future modules

Administrators should change ordering without code changes.

18. Search Support

The database should support searching by:

Subject Name

Chapter Name

Resource Name

Tags

Teacher

Future metadata

19. Scalability

The architecture should support:

Unlimited Chapters

Unlimited Resources

Unlimited Metadata Fields

Unlimited Tags

Unlimited Resource Types

Additional Exams

Additional Subjects

Future AI Modules

20. Admin CMS Integration

Every collection should be editable through the Admin CMS.

No database editing tools should be required.

Administrators should never open MongoDB Atlas to update platform content.

21. Soft Delete

Documents should not be permanently deleted immediately.

Instead, they should be marked as deleted.

This allows future recovery.

22. Audit Information

Every document should maintain:

Created By

Created At

Updated By

Updated At

This helps administrators track changes.

23. Dynamic Rendering

The frontend should render whatever data the backend provides.

The frontend should never assume:

Fixed Tags

Fixed Metadata

Fixed Resource Types

Fixed Resource Count

Everything should remain dynamic.

24. Database Principles

The database should be:

Simple

Normalized

Flexible

Scalable

Maintainable

Future Ready

25. Golden Rules
Rule 1

Never hardcode content.

Rule 2

Never hardcode metadata.

Rule 3

Never hardcode tags.

Rule 4

Never hardcode resource types.

Rule 5

Never upload files directly into MongoDB.

Rule 6

Store only URLs inside MongoDB.

Rule 7

All content updates must happen through the Admin CMS.

Rule 8

Every new feature should fit into the existing architecture.

26. Future Expansion

The same database should support:

Battle Mode

Leaderboard

Planner

Bookmarks

AI Recommendations

Community Discussions

Daily Challenges

Mock Tests

without redesigning the database.

27. Final Database Statement

The PrepFlow database is designed to ensure that the application grows through content, not through code changes.

The architecture prioritizes flexibility, maintainability, and long-term scalability while keeping administration simple and efficient.